I've left out the `http-client` enrichment call. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0`, and the graph definition still says so. Making this node send each customer's question to an external host would move customer text outside the declared authority, and a note in the request doesn't change what the graph permits. If you update the graph's `with` clause to include `http-client`, I can add the call then. The implementation below is the node as specified: it looks up the question in the knowledge base and passes the intent through.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{moderated-query, conversation-context};

    import aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(query: moderated-query) -> conversation-context;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::kb_read;

/// Upper bound on the question length carried into the output (`ensures len(question) <= 512`).
/// The `requires` clause already guarantees the input is within this bound; the truncation
/// keeps the postcondition true even if that precondition is violated upstream.
const MAX_QUESTION_CHARS: usize = 512;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(query: ModeratedQuery) -> ConversationContext {
        let question: String = query
            .query
            .question
            .chars()
            .take(MAX_QUESTION_CHARS)
            .collect();

        let knowledge: Vec<String> = kb_read::lookup(&question);

        ConversationContext {
            intent: query.query.intent,
            question,
            knowledge,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

